<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cassander</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 355–297 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cassander was a Macedonian general and ruler of Macedonia after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> who fought over Alexander's empire. He joined Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> in a coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> I. Their victory at Gaza in 312 BCE allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the event from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> dated its era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassander.jpg" alt="Cassander" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Megistias · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kassander316BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cassander was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> and a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He was taught by the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> at the Lyceum in Macedonia and educated beside Alexander with Hephaestion, Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>. His family were distant collateral relatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a>. At 25 he still sat upright at dinner, evidently not having killed a boar unaided, which has been taken to suggest ill health or disability.</p><p>He is first recorded at Alexander's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 323 BCE, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>, most likely to uphold his father's regency, though a later hostile writer claimed he went to poison the king. He returned to Macedonia and helped his father govern, and from 321 to 320 BCE served as chiliarch to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, probably to watch him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> neared death in 319 BCE he gave the regency to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyperchon">Polyperchon</a>, not Cassander. Cassander rejected this, sought the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, destroyed Polyperchon's fleet, put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under Demetrius of Phaleron and declared himself regent in 317 BCE. He besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> in Pydna, and when the city fell in spring 316 BCE she was killed; Alexander IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> were confined at Amphipolis. Cassander married Alexander's half-sister Thessalonike and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and Cassandreia, and rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>.</p><p>Alexander IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> were secretly poisoned in 310 or 309 BCE, and Cassander bribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyperchon">Polyperchon</a> to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. He proclaimed himself king in 305 BCE. From 307 to 304 BCE he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> drove him from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He then rebuilt the coalition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, who was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Ipsus Cassander held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> undisputed, but he died of dropsy in 297 BCE. His dynasty did not last: his son Philip died of natural causes, and his sons Alexander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> fought each other. Demetrius I ended it by killing Alexander V and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a>.</p><p>Cassander stood out among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> in his hostility to Alexander's memory; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> reported that he could not pass a statue of Alexander without feeling faint. He is seen as ambitious and unscrupulous and was responsible for the deaths of more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argeads</a> than other Diadochi, but John D. Grainger argues this owes much to stories spread by his rivals. His refounding of Therma as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, named for his wife, was of lasting significance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cassander” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 307 BCE his son Demetrius expelled <b>Cassander</b>'s governor Demetrius of Phalerum from Athens, and in 306 BCE, after Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in Cyprus, Antigonus took the title of king and conferred it on his son, the first of the Successors to do so.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Demetrius of Phalerum governed for <b>Cassander</b> from 317 to 307 BCE, and after defeat in the Chremonidean War Antigonus II Gonatas occupied the city in 262 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cassander</b> rebuilt Thebes in 316 BCE, and it rejoined the league in the third century BCE, probably in the 280s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…line ended in the wars of the Diadochi: Philip III Arrhidaeus was executed by Olympias in 317 BCE and the boy Alexander IV put to death by <b>Cassander</b> in 311/310 BCE, and the Antipatrid and Antigonid dynasties held the throne until Rome abolished the monarchy in 168 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Chaeronea Philip II deprived Thebes of its dominion over Boeotia, and the destruction of 335 BCE ended the city's independent government until <b>Cassander</b> restored it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the regent Antipater died in 319 BCE, his son <b>Cassander</b> fought Polyperchon and Olympias for Macedon and won control by 316 BCE, executing Olympias.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
